title: combine
---



```python
combine(datasets, scale, years=None, extra=(), rules=None, ref=None)
```

Merge datasets into one panel at ``scale``, aggregating finer ones up.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name     | Type         | Description                                                      | Default    |
|----------|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| datasets |              | Dataset ids, or ``{dataset: [variables]}`` to pick variables     | _required_ |
| scale    | str          | Target scale; each dataset must be published at it or nest in it | _required_ |
| years    |              | As in ``geo.load``                                               | `None`     |
| extra    |              | Your own panels (with ``geo_id`` and ``year``) at ``scale``      | `()`       |
| rules    | dict \| None | Aggregation rules overriding the metadata, ``{variable: rule}``  | `None`     |

## Notes {.doc-section .doc-section-notes}

Variables without an aggregation rule (``none``) are dropped, with a warning,
when a dataset has to be aggregated. Clashing column names get the dataset
id as a prefix (``<dataset>_<column>``). Weighted means fetch their weights as
``geo.aggregate()`` does (``geo:weights`` in the metadata).

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo
import plotly.express as px

dep = geo.combine(["sdg03", "sdg04"], scale="department", years=2024)
dep = geo.add_names(dep)
px.scatter(dep, x="index_sdg3", y="index_sdg4", text="name",
           labels=geo.labels(dep), title="Health and education, ~2024")
```

